Dross-Liturgy

Ingredients: THRASH METAL + TECH HOUSE + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Tenor Saxophone
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from a desert caravan culture that trades in salvaged synthesizers.

Dross-Liturgy is a punishing, dust-caked collision where hyper-precise thrash metal gallops and bleak cathedral drones are hammered into the relentless four-on-the-floor roll of tech house, punctuated by a distorted, overblown tenor saxophone that wails like a dying war horn across irradiated dunes.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 126–130 BPM. The pulse relies on a hypnotic, rolling tech-house sub-kick and shuffle-hat foundation, abruptly sliced open by 32nd-note thrash alternate picking and double-kick blast-skanks executed at double-time velocity without ever breaking the underlying four-four dance pocket.
- Harmonic Palette: Locrian and Phrygian Dominant modes laced with chromatic thrash runs, diminished desert scales, and monastic Gothic chants that pivot around subterranean root pedals in C# minor and D minor.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Silt-Chamber Overdrive: Running salvaged digital synthesizer oscillators through cracked Soviet germanium diodes and re-amping them inside steel shipping containers filled with loose desert sand to create gritty, unpredictable harmonics.
  2. Banshee-Reed Processing: Routing the tenor saxophone through high-gain octave fuzz, pitch shifters, and aggressive sidechain ducking so it cuts through the wall of metal guitars like an abrasive, shrieking lead synthesizer.
  3. Anvil-Gated Sidechaining: Crushing down-tuned, palm-muted thrash guitars against the four-on-the-floor kick drum using hyper-fast optical compression, yielding a pneumatic, suction-like pumping groove.
- Dynamics: Monolithic, hypnotic build-ups constructed from looping percussion and low saxophone drones that abruptly detonate into frantic, serrated thrash riffs and feral vocal incantations before collapsing back into dark, skeletal house grooves.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born amidst the wandering, solar-rigged freight convoys of the Great Caliche Basin, Dross-Liturgy arose from nomadic scrap-scavengers who trade in pre-collapse synthesizers, burnt rack units, and copper wiring salvaged from forgotten military bunkers. Fashion in this scene is functional yet theatrical: billowing dust-cowls of sun-bleached linen layered over studded leather motorcycle cuirasses, goggles fitted with cracked optical filters, and brass sax mouthpieces worn on heavy silver rosary chains around the neck. The ritual epicenter is the nocturnal Kharvost—a trade moot where flatbed diesel trucks form a perimeter against the sandstorms, generators roar into life, and musicians wage high-decibel sonic duels under skeletal iron scaffolds, bartering obsolete circuit boards and rhythm modules using cassette tapes as currency.

Typical Instruments
- Central Reeds: Vintage tenor saxophone retrofitted with piezo contact mics, envelope-following auto-wahs, and dual high-gain distortion stompboxes.
- Strings & Metal: 7-string down-tuned electric guitars (tuned to Drop-A) played with razor-sharp palm muting, aggressive thrash down-picking, and whammy dive-bombs.
- Salvaged Electronics: Circuit-bent FM synthesizers (rescued Yamaha TX81Z and DX units), Roland Juno clones with damaged voice chips, analog sequencer pedals, and modular filters choked with dry mineral silt.
- Percussion: Heavy four-on-the-floor analog sub-kicks, metallic scrap percussion (pipe hits, spent shell casings), and synthetic tech-house shaker loops layered over acoustic blast-beat snares.

For Fans Of: Slayer, Eli Brown, Colin Stetson, The Sisters of Mercy

