Track 3: Kharvost-Gate of Bir Zirwah
- Story & Context: Lore among the badland caravaneers recounts that whoever crosses the monoliths of Bir Zirwah when the solstice clock strikes noon will encounter the petrified remains of the convoy that vanished chasing a phantom radio transmission. When scrap divers finally uncovered the fossilized caravan in the limestone trench, they discovered three skeletons still clutching their instruments—their tenor saxophone jammed tight with calcified hornets and the synthesizer oscillators still droning from infinite solar feedback. The recorded loop recovered from their tape deck is this track: a relentless, sinister Gothic rave where relentless thrash chugs battle a hypnotic sub-bass shuffle until the sand consumes the rhythm. Scratched onto the faceplate of the lead rig’s console was the final surviving covenant: "We buried the calendar beneath ten tons of copper wire."
